{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>Bezer in the wilderness.</b> And written after it is ״וזאת התורה״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in tractate Makkot, chapter ״ואלו הן הגולין״: from here that words of Torah are a refuge, even from the angel of death. As in the case of Rav Hisda, who was sitting and reciting in the academy of Rav, and the angel of death was unable to approach him because his mouth did not cease from his recitation. He [the angel of death] went up onto the cedar of the academy of Rav; the cedar split, he [Rav Hisda] fell silent, and he [the angel of death] was able to take him. And there [in that same passage] Resh Lakish further said: the prince of Rome is destined to make three errors, as it is written (Isaiah 63:1): ״מי זה בא מאדום חמוץ בגדים מבצרה״ — he erred, for it does not give refuge except Bezer in the wilderness, and he fled to Bozrah; he erred, for it does not give refuge except to one who killed unintentionally, and he killed intentionally; he erred, for it does not give refuge except to a human, and he was an angel." "<b>In the land of the plain, for the Reubenites.</b> In the portion of Reuben. What is the reason? Because Reuben was first to begin with the saving of lives, as it is said (Genesis 37:21): ״וישמע ראובן ויצילהו מידם״, and it is written (ibid.): ״ויאמר אליהם ראובן אל תשפכו דם״; therefore the cities of refuge were set apart in the portion of Reuben. And this is what is written (Proverbs 28:17): ״אדם עשוק בדם נפש עד בור ינוס אל יתמכו בו״ — ״עד בור״, until the boundary of Reuben, about whom it is written (Genesis 37:29): ״וישב ראובן אל הבור״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:44.174732850Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}